  "notes": [
    "Ticketmaster's explanation (Nov 19, 2022) of the Eras Tour Verified Fan onsale (Nov 15): 3.5 million pre-registrations, ~1.5 million invited, \"3.5 billion total system requests – 4x our previous peak\", a \"staggering\" number of bot attacks, about 15% of interactions had issues (including passcode validation errors that made some fans lose tickets in their carts); still, over 2 million tickets sold that day.",
    "The atomic step is a conditional update: UPDATE seats SET status='sold' WHERE id='A12' AND status='free', then check the affected row count. Alternatives: a row lock (SELECT … FOR UPDATE) in a transaction, or a version column (optimistic locking).",
    "Holds: a held_until timestamp on the seat (reads treat expired holds as free), or a key with a TTL in Redis. The expiry job keeps the data clean; the hold length is a product decision (often a few minutes).",
    "Payments: confirm the hold before charging, and make the payment call idempotent so a retry never charges twice.",
    "Virtual waiting rooms (e.g. Queue-it): over-capacity visitors wait on a separate page; for scheduled sales, early visitors are randomized at the start; people are let in at a configured rate and come back with a signed token. A check done only in the browser can be skipped; checks at the server, CDN or load balancer can't.",
    "Bots: pre-registration (like Verified Fan), CAPTCHAs, rate limits per account and device, purchase limits per person.",
    "More servers do help the read side (seat maps can be cached and served from a CDN); the contention is on the write: each seat can be sold once.",
    "Fairness: random order is fair to everyone who arrived before the start; first come, first served rewards the fastest connection, the most tabs, and bots.",
    "Scaling out works because the backend is stateless: any copy can serve any request, so a load balancer can spread traffic with no sticky sessions. Session or cart state lives in the database (or a shared cache), not in the server's memory.",
    "The seat map is read-heavy: serve it from a CDN or a cache with a short TTL (seconds). Showing a seat as free for a second after it was taken is acceptable because the conditional write is the source of truth; the fan just gets 'taken, pick another'.",
    "Why not shard the database? Sharding by event spreads different events, but one huge event is a single hot partition: thousands of buyers fight over the same rows. You can split by section, but the contention per seat remains, which is why admission control (the waiting room) is the standard answer.",
    "Read replicas help the read side too, but a replica can't take the purchase: writes for a seat must go to one place to stay consistent.",
    "Payment races: store who holds the seat and until when (held_by, held_until). Confirm with a conditional update (sold WHERE held_by = this fan AND held_until > now). If the hold was lost while the payment was processing, refund. Many sites also extend the hold while a payment is in progress.",
    "Payments are asynchronous too: providers confirm with webhooks that can arrive late or twice, so deduplicate by event id. Use an idempotency key per payment attempt (the provider returns the first result), and a reconciliation job that compares payments with seats to catch the leftovers (charged but not sold, or sold but not charged).",
    "Group purchases: hold the seats in one transaction, all or nothing. When several buyers lock overlapping seats, lock them in a consistent order (for example sorted by seat id) to avoid deadlocks.",
    "Capacity: 100,000 fans x 20 refreshes per minute = about 33,000 requests per second; this is an illustrative example, not Ticketmaster's data. The point is the ratio: almost all requests are reads (cacheable), while the writes compete for a fixed set of seats. Size for the peak, not the average.",
    "Waiting room hardening: serve the room as a static page from a CDN so it does not fall over itself; tokens are signed, short-lived and bound to one fan; one place per account or device so opening ten tabs gives ten chances to nobody. Once inventory hits zero, serve a cached 'sold out' page so the system stops doing seat checks.",
    "General admission (no numbered seats): a counter with an atomic decrement (for example Redis DECR) can replace per-seat rows; the rule is the same, one writer decides and the check and the write are one step."
  ],
